If LaTeX: X and LaTeX: Y are random variables and LaTeX: a is a non-random constant:
LaTeX: \mbox{E}[aX]= a\mbox{E}[X]
LaTeX: \mbox{E}[X + Y] = \mbox{E}[X] + \mbox{E}[Y]
LaTeX: \mbox{SD}[X] = \sqrt{\mbox{Var}[X]}
LaTeX: \mbox{SD}[aX] = a\mbox{SD}[X]
If LaTeX: X and LaTeX: Y are independent, LaTeX: \mbox{Var}[X + Y] = \mbox{Var}[X] + \mbox{Var}[Y].
You have LaTeX: n observations LaTeX: (x_1, y_1), \dots (x_N,y_N) where each pair is a realization of random variables LaTeX: X and LaTeX: Y .
Notation reminder: LaTeX: \bar{y} and LaTeX: \bar{x} are the sample averages, LaTeX: s_Y and LaTeX: s_X are the sample standard deviations, and LaTeX: r the sample correlation.
Sample correlation is defined as LaTeX: 
r = \frac{1}{N-1}\sum_{i=1}^N
\left(\frac{x_i - \bar{x}}{s_x}\right)
\left(\frac{y_i - \bar{y}}{s_y}\right)
The regression line for predicting LaTeX: Y given LaTeX: X=x is LaTeX: \frac{\hat{Y} - \bar{y}}{s_Y} =  r\frac{x - \bar{x}}{s_X}
The standard error of the sample correlation is LaTeX: \sqrt{\frac{1 - \rho^2}{N - 2}} with LaTeX: \rho the true correlation of LaTeX: X
 and LaTeX: Y.
